"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,294\nWise feedback\n\n4\n00:00:12,094 --> 00:00:14,769\nCritical feedback is how students improve.\n\n5\n00:00:15,223 --> 00:00:17,018\nBut it can be read two ways.\n\n6\n00:00:17,376 --> 00:00:19,417\nMy teacher thinks I can do better.\n\n7\n00:00:19,689 --> 00:00:22,895\nOr, my teacher thinks people like me can't do this.\n\n8\n00:00:23,212 --> 00:00:25,932\nFor students who expect to be stereotyped,\n\n9\n00:00:25,952 --> 00:00:27,607\nthat ambiguity is real.\n\n10\n00:00:27,777 --> 00:00:29,146\nIn a middle school study,\n\n11\n00:00:29,252 --> 00:00:32,515\nseventh graders wrote essays about a personal hero,\n\n12\n00:00:32,781 --> 00:00:34,365\nand their teachers marked them up.\n\n13\n00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:42,048\nSome students also got a handwritten note: I'm giving you these comments because I have very high expectations,\n\n14\n00:00:42,262 --> 00:00:43,947\nand I know that you can reach them.\n\n15\n00:00:44,547 --> 00:00:46,210\nOthers got a neutral note.\n\n16\n00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:48,656\nAmong African American students,\n\n17\n00:00:48,885 --> 00:00:53,926\nabout seventy percent of those who got the high expectations note chose to revise,\n\n18\n00:00:54,174 --> 00:00:56,918\ncompared with seventeen percent of those who didn't.\n\n19\n00:00:57,569 --> 00:00:59,985\nAmong those who most distrusted school,\n\n20\n00:01:00,235 --> 00:01:02,117\neighty two percent revised,\n\n21\n00:01:02,275 --> 00:01:03,506\ncompared with none.\n\n22\n00:01:03,676 --> 00:01:04,808\nIn a second study,\n\n23\n00:01:04,995 --> 00:01:06,500\nrevision was required.\n\n24\n00:01:07,096 --> 00:01:12,033\nEighty eight percent of African American students with the note improved their scores,\n\n25\n00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:14,693\ncompared with thirty four percent without.\n\n26\n00:01:15,267 --> 00:01:17,542\nWhite students also revised more,\n\n27\n00:01:17,772 --> 00:01:20,036\nbut that difference was not significant.\n\n28\n00:01:20,206 --> 00:01:22,158\nThe note did two things at once.\n\n29\n00:01:22,405 --> 00:01:24,297\nIt held the bar high,\n\n30\n00:01:24,593 --> 00:01:25,952\nand it said: I believe you can reach it.\n\n31\n00:01:26,449 --> 00:01:29,241\nEarlier research found that praise alone,\n\n32\n00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:30,734\nor high standards alone,\n\n33\n00:01:30,754 --> 00:01:31,939\nworked less well.\n\n34\n00:01:32,109 --> 00:01:33,094\nSome caution.\n\n35\n00:01:33,495 --> 00:01:34,893\nThese studies were small,\n\n36\n00:01:34,913 --> 00:01:37,138\nwith a few dozen students per group.\n\n37\n00:01:37,427 --> 00:01:39,266\nAnd the message has to be true.\n\n38\n00:01:39,632 --> 00:01:43,265\nIt only works if you really do help students reach the standard.\n\n39\n00:01:43,435 --> 00:01:44,234\nFor teaching.\n\n40\n00:01:44,515 --> 00:01:46,646\nState your high standards plainly.\n\n41\n00:01:46,938 --> 00:01:49,524\nSay that you believe each student can reach them.\n\n42\n00:01:49,916 --> 00:01:52,729\nGive specific next steps,\n\n43\n00:01:52,899 --> 00:01:54,922\nnot just a grade.\n\n44\n00:01:55,312 --> 00:01:57,218\nAnd build in time to revise.\n\n45\n00:01:57,506 --> 00:02:01,826\nOne tip to share with students: tough feedback is a sign of high standards,\n\n46\n00:02:01,846 --> 00:02:02,127\nnot low expectations.\n\n47\n00:02:02,297 --> 00:02:05,307\nHigh standards, and belief that students can reach them.\n\n48\n00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:06,553\nSay both.\n\n"
